<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter11_constructors-destructors-and-raii">
    <title>Constructors, Destructors, and RAII During Exceptions</title>

    <p>
        Exceptions can interrupt normal control flow, but C++ still performs
        required object cleanup. Objects with automatic storage are destroyed
        as their scopes are left. This behavior supports
        <term>RAII</term>, or Resource Acquisition Is Initialization.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_destructor-during-unwinding">
        <title>Destructors Run During Stack Unwinding</title>

        <p>
            If an object has been completely constructed before an exception is
            thrown, its destructor runs when its scope is exited during stack
            unwinding. An object whose constructor throws is not considered a
            fully constructed object, so its complete destructor does not run.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter11_destructor-unwinding">
            <title>Cleanup during an exception</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter11_destructor-unwinding"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

class ScopeMarker {
public:
    ScopeMarker() {
        std::cout << "created" << '\n';
    }

    ~ScopeMarker() {
        std::cout << "destroyed" << '\n';
    }
};

void run_task() {
    ScopeMarker marker;
    throw std::runtime_error("task failed");
}

int main() {
    try {
        run_task();
    }
    catch (const std::exception& error) {
        std::cout << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The expected order includes <c>destroyed</c> before the handler
            prints the error. The local object is cleaned up as
            <c>run_task()</c> is exited.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_raii-purpose">
        <title>Why RAII Helps</title>

        <p>
            With RAII, a resource is acquired by an object's constructor and
            released by its destructor. The release happens when the object
            leaves its scope, whether the scope ends normally or because an
            exception is propagating.
        </p>

        <p>
            This is safer than asking every possible control-flow path to call a
            separate cleanup function. The standard library uses this design for
            many objects, including strings, vectors, streams, and smart
            pointers.
        </p>

        <p>
            A destructor should normally perform cleanup and should not allow a
            second exception to escape while another exception is already being
            handled. Later chapters will apply RAII to dynamically allocated
            resources and smart pointers.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter11_raii-visualization">
        <title>Tracing RAII Cleanup</title>

        <p>
            In the static listing, <c>marker</c> is completely constructed before the exception is thrown. During stack unwinding, its destructor runs before the handler reports the failure.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter11_raii-exercise-order">
        <statement><p>What happens to <c>marker</c> when <c>run_task()</c> throws?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It remains alive until the operating system shuts down.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Automatic objects are destroyed when their scope is exited.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Its constructor runs a second time.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>An exception does not reconstruct the local object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It is copied into the exception object.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The local object's lifetime is separate from the exception object.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>Its destructor is skipped because an exception is active.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>Automatic objects are destroyed during stack unwinding.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Its destructor runs while the function is being exited.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Stack unwinding destroys completely constructed local objects.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_raii-parsons-cleanup">
        <statement><p>Arrange the class so its destructor releases the resource marker.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>class ScopeMarker {</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>public:</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>ScopeMarker() { acquire_resource(); }</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>~ScopeMarker() { release_resource(); }</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>};</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_raii-exercise-purpose">
        <statement><p>What is the main advantage of RAII during exception handling?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>RAII prevents all exceptions from being thrown.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>RAII manages cleanup; it does not prevent every failure.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Cleanup happens automatically when the resource-owning object leaves its scope.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>RAII makes cleanup apply to both normal and exceptional exits.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>RAII requires every resource to be released manually in <c>main()</c>.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>RAII connects resource cleanup to object lifetime and scope.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>RAII makes destructors run before constructors.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Construction still occurs before destruction.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>RAII removes the need for object scopes.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>RAII depends on object lifetimes and scopes.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter11_raii-exercise-destructor">
        <statement><p>Which destructor practice is safest during exception handling?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Delete every object in the program.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A destructor should clean up only its own object's resources.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Always throw a new exception from the destructor.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Throwing from a destructor during unwinding is dangerous.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Perform cleanup and avoid allowing another exception to escape.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A second escaping exception during unwinding can terminate the program.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Leave resources active so the handler can clean them later.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>RAII is intended to make cleanup automatic and reliable.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                            <statement><p>Allow the destructor to throw whenever cleanup is difficult.</p></statement>
                            <feedback><p>Throwing during cleanup can cause termination during stack unwinding.</p></feedback>
                        </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
